const intro={"n":13,"title":"The Younger Son's Childhood, Inheritance and Guardianship","nums":[721,724,726],"summary":"A different son of Martín Fierro begins telling his story. He recalls the family being scattered after the loss of its head and says that an aunt eventually took him in, cared for him and made him the heir to her cattle and sheep. When she died, a judge intervened, declaring him too young to manage the property and arranging a guardian. The young man describes his subsequent poverty and the loss of security. His account leads directly toward the appointment of the man called Viejo Vizcacha.","people":"Martín Fierro's younger son as first-person speaker; the caring aunt; a judge who controls the inheritance; a manager or guardian introduced through the legal arrangement.","themes":"Family separation; care and intergenerational bonds; inheritance and legal power; childhood vulnerability; dependence on guardians.","words":"tía (aunt); heredero (heir); rodeo (herd of cattle in this context); majada (flock of sheep); tutor (guardian); chiripá (wrapped garment).","questions":["What happens to children when the family is dispersed, according to the opening image?","How does the aunt change the younger son's life?","What property does she leave him?","What reason does the judge give for assigning a guardian?","What warning signs appear before Viejo Vizcacha enters the story?"],"analysis":"This canto begins the younger son's testimony, not a further chapter of the father's imprisonment. It offers a contrast between care freely given by the aunt and the institutional guardianship imposed after her death. We should not claim that the text proves a particular legal fraud beyond what the young narrator reports and suspects.","related":"/en/library/old-vizcacha-vs-martin-fierro/","relatedTitle":"Compare Vizcacha's later advice with Fierro's"};
